= Introduction <introduction>


There is a well-established discrepancy between transcriptomic and proteomic measurements, whereby only approximately 40% of the variation observed at the protein level can be explained by corresponding mRNA expression levels @vogel2012. Although proteins are synthesized from transcripts and both ultimately originate from the genomic blueprint, multiple regulatory layers—including translational control and protein degradation—contribute to this divergence @crick1970.


Understanding these molecular differences is particularly important in glioblastoma multiforme (GBM), the most aggressive primary brain tumour in adults, which remains associated with poor patient survival despite advances in treatment @lemee2018. In addition to extensive molecular heterogeneity between patients, GBM can be classified into distinct molecular subtypes that exhibit characteristic transcriptional programmes and biological behaviours. Advances in high-throughput transcriptomic and proteomic technologies, together with AI-driven analytical methods, have enabled increasingly comprehensive investigations of these subtype-specific molecular profiles @singh2022. Interpreting these data within their biological context is therefore essential for identifying dysregulated pathways and potential therapeutic targets.

This thesis investigates transcriptomic and proteomic alterations across GBM molecular subtypes through differential expression analysis relative to healthy brain tissue. Functional interpretation of the resulting differentially expressed genes and proteins is performed using Gene Ontology enrichment analysis, followed by integration with genes involved in regulated cell death pathways, including apoptosis, autophagy, ferroptosis, necroptosis, and pyroptosis. By combining differential expression with functional enrichment and pathway-based analyses, this work aims to provide insight into subtype-specific biological processes and the potential roles of regulated cell death mechanisms in GBM.

Chapter @background presents the relevant knowledge regarding the thesis's topic including glioblastoma and multiomics of glioblastoma. Chapter @methodology shows the retrieval and processing of data concerning differential analyses of GBM against healthy brain tissue in both transcriptome and proteome.